"killed": {"parent": 0, "user": 0, "system": 0}, "refused": {"depth_limit": 0, "concurrency_limit": 0, "budget": 0}, "by_type": {}}, "safety_stops": 0, "is_error": false, "num_turns": 57, "subtype": "success", "api_error_status": null, "result": "I built the registrar model from `rules.md`. `gnd validate` passes on the full model (exit 0, no warnings), so both structural and semantic layers were checked.\n\nI didn't ask which CSDP mode you wanted. Your file is a complete description, so I ran One-shot mode. If you want to go through the open questions below one at a time, say so and I'll switch to Guided.\n\n**Files** (in the working directory):\n- `model.orm.json`: the model, named the way `rules.md` asked (the skill's default name is `model.orm.yaml`).\n- `references/catalog.pop.json`: the spring catalogue as a fixed reference population, tied to the model by its SHA-256.\n- `model.verbalization.txt`: the whole model in plain sentences for the registrar to review, with the open questions listed.\n\n**What's in it:** 9 fact types and 5 operations (enroll, drop a course, drop a term, record a grade, admit a student). Every rule in your file is in the model as a hard constraint:\n- Prerequisites are checked through the whole chain. A derived \"requires\" relation follows prerequisites of prerequisites, so CS340 also needs CS101 and MATH120.\n- A student can't enroll in a course they've already passed.\n- At most 6 courses per term.\n- At most 18 credits per term. This uses a derived per-term credit total.\n- One grade per course, and a recorded grade never changes.\n- Only courses offered that term can be taken.\n\n**Tested on Maya's history:**\n- **Breaking case:** enrolled in CS340, CS230 and CS250 in spring, 21 credits in total. The checker caught all four problems: missing MATH220 and STAT210, CS230 already passed, CS250 not offered in spring, and the credit cap.\n- **Legal case:** MATH220, STAT210 and PHIL101, 10 credits. Zero failures. The run exits 2 rather than 0 only because there are no grades yet, so the one-grade rule had nothing to check.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- **`gnd` bug:** my first version of the credit total crashed `gnd check` with an internal error (exit 70). I changed how it's derived to work around it. The catch is that a population now has to state each student's credit total for the 18-credit rule to run. If it leaves the total out, the check reports \"indeterminate\" rather than passing, so nothing slips through silently. This is worth reporting upstream.\n- **Downstream tools:** `gnd synth` and `gnd codegen` don't accept a model with a fixed catalogue. If you need them, the catalogue can be turned into ordinary facts instead.\n- **My own inferences are advisory only.** These are: prerequisites can't form a loop, every course has one title and one credit value, and every graded course counts as passed. None of them can fail a check until you confirm them.\n\n**Questions for the registrar:**\n1. If an operation changes something it didn't declare, should that be a warning or a hard failure? It has to be stated either way, so I set warning for every operation.\n2. Recording a grade removes the enrollment, so \"enrolled\" means \"currently registered\". I chose this so that \"no enrolling in a passed course\" doesn't clash with keeping grades forever. Is that right?\n3. Can a grade be a fail? As written, every recorded grade marks the course as passed.\n4. Are students identified by name, or do they have a student number?\n5. Terms are just \"spring\" and \"fall\", with no year. Should a term carry a year?", "ttft_ms": 2146, "type": "result", "duration_ms": 429083, "uuid": "17d63e4a-a426-45a9-a81c-6ee00839527c", "ttft_stream_ms": 1593, "time_to_request_ms": 33, "first_content_frame_ms": 1593, "queued_turn_count": 0, "result_index": 0} 
